          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Goldbach’s conjecture",
              "marks": [
                "bold"
              ],
              "colour": "#ef6068"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " states that every even number greater than 2 can be expressed as the sum of two prime numbers.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p5-q13-task-paragraph",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Show that this is true for the even numbers between 140 and 160.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "One possible set is:\n$$\\begin{aligned}140&=3+137\\\\142&=3+139\\\\144&=5+139\\\\146&=7+139\\\\148&=11+137\\\\150&=11+139\\\\152&=13+139\\\\154&=17+137\\\\156&=17+139\\\\158&=19+139\\\\160&=11+149\\end{aligned}$$\nEach summand is prime. Other prime pairs are possible.",
      "worked": "Choose a prime and check whether the remaining amount is also prime.\n$$\\begin{align*}140&=3+137\\\\142&=3+139\\\\144&=5+139\\\\146&=7+139\\\\148&=11+137\\\\150&=11+139\\\\152&=13+139\\\\154&=17+137\\\\156&=17+139\\\\158&=19+139\\\\160&=11+149\\end{align*}$$\nThe smaller summands $3,5,7,11,13,17,19$ are prime. None of $137,139,149$ is divisible by $2,3,5,7$ or $11$. Any composite number this small would have a prime factor among these, so all three are prime. Thus every even number from $140$ to $160$, including both endpoints, is a sum of two primes.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
